{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310759,"nonce":"fjO0VNJFylmjJsytHNijdQv3KRY=","title":"Xml COMPILES after @:isVar, but Xml.parse still fails on THREE separate blockers, each isolated with a minimal repro: a block-init static ...","message":"After [[feature-isvar-backing-storage]], `Xml` compiles (its 8 `index not found\nfor write` errors are gone). `Xml.parse(\"\u003ca\u003e\u003cb/\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\")` still does not work, and\nit is **three independent defects**, not one.\n\n\n`haxe/xml/Parser.hx:100`:\n\n```haxe\nstatic var escapes = {\n    var h = new haxe.ds.StringMap();\n    h.set(\"lt\", \"\u003c\"); ... h;\n}\n```\n\n`RAYZOR_DEBUG_RECV=1` on the failing compile prints, three times:\n\n```\n[recv] method=exists receiver_type=Some(\"Dynamic\") stdlib_class=None\n```\n\nso the block-initializer's type never reaches the static, and `exists` then\nmatches four map classes -\u003e `E0801 ambiguous dynamic method dispatch`. Adding\n`:haxe.ds.StringMap\u003cString\u003e` to the declaration makes the whole module compile —\nthat is the PROOF, **not the fix**: haxe-std should stay upstream-shaped and\nthis is an inference bug.\n\n\n**The Parser SOURCE is fine.** `scratchpad/z1` copies the whole file byte-for-byte\ninto a project as `hx.xml.Zarser` (only the class names changed). It **compiles\nand runs**; the only E0801 in that run belongs to the stdlib `haxe.xml.Parser`,\npulled in behind `Xml`. Identical source, different module identity.\n\nThe difference is that `Zarser` is NOT in a cycle: `Xml` still points at\n`haxe.xml.Parser`, not at it. On the first pass `haxe.xml.Parser` is compiled\nbefore `Xml`, `Xml` is unresolved throughout its body, and inference collapses —\ntaking `escapes` with it, even though `escapes` is a `StringMap` with no\nrelation to `Xml`. It is compiled **three times** (pass + two retries, visible\nwith `RAYZOR_GLOBALS_DEBUG=1` as three `@g0 Parser.escapes` blocks) and fails\nidentically each time, so this is not stale-retry state.\n\nSo blockers 1 and 2 have ONE root cause: the `Xml` \u003c-\u003e `haxe.xml.Parser` cycle.\nFix cycle support and both should go.\n\n**Ruled out by experiment**, each with a probe: single module; inside a package;\na second class ahead of it; the byte-identical initializer including the\n`'\"'`/`\"'\"` literals; placed INSIDE haxe-std; a 2-deep import chain;\n`using StringTools`; a `private enum abstract` ahead of it; an `Xml`-typed\nparameter; and **the class name** — renaming the stdlib `class Parser` to\n`XmlDocParser` (updating `Xml.hx:122`) does NOT fix it.\n\n**METHOD WARNING — my first bisection was invalid.** Every variant used a class\nnamed `Parser`, which collides with the already-failing stdlib module, so all 25+\nof them failed for that reason no matter what was removed. That is why removing\neach of the 18 `case S.*` branches, the `#if !(target.unicode)` block, the\nshadowing `var c`, the `'\u0026$s;'` interpolation, `using StringTools`, the `S`\nabstract, `XmlParserException`, `parse`, `isValidChar`, and finally reducing\n`doParse` to five lines ALL still \"failed\". **Use a non-colliding name when\ncopying a stdlib module for bisection**, or every result is a false positive.\n\n\nMinimal, plain Haxe, no properties and no statics involved\n(`scratchpad/c1`) — the oracle prints `parsed: escaped / parsed: zz`:\n\n```haxe\n// Doc.hx\nclass Doc {\n    public var name:String;\n    public function new(n:String) this.name = n;\n    public static function parse(s:String):Doc return a.b.Esc.parse(s);\n}\n// a/b/Esc.hx\npackage a.b;\nclass Esc {\n    public static function parse(s:String):Doc {\n        var d:Doc = null; d = new Doc(s);\n        if (s == \"lt\") return new Doc(\"escaped\");\n        return d;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**exit 139, silently.** `Xml` \u003c-\u003e `haxe.xml.Parser` is exactly this shape, and\n`Xml.parse` still exits 139 even with blocker 1 annotated away — so this is what\nis left after the E0801.\n\n**Verified PRE-EXISTING**, not a consequence of the `@:isVar` work: the same\nrepro exits 139 on a build of `23b218c1` (the commit before it). Checked by\nrebuilding at that commit, not by arguing that a layout change \"cannot\" affect a\nprogram with no properties.\n\n\n```rust\n// No constructor plus exactly one argument is a value wrap; this catches\n// abstract types not detected above.\nif !has_constructor \u0026\u0026 args.len() == 1 {\n    let result = self.lower_expression(\u0026args[0]);\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\nUnder a cycle no constructor resolves, so `new Doc(s)` becomes **`s`**. MIR for\nthe broken `a.b.Esc.count` has no alloc and no ctor call at all — it GEPs into\nthe string parameter. `RAYZOR_CTOR_DEBUG=1` prints\n`[CTOR] class=Doc path=bare-name fid=Some(..)` in the working case and **nothing**\nin the broken one, because this early return fires first.\n\nRepro matrix (`scratchpad/k1..k6`, `c1`), oracle in every case:\n| | no cycle | cycle |\n|---|---|---|\n| `return new Doc(s)` | k1 OK | k4 **SIGSEGV** |\n| `var d = new Doc(s)` | k3 OK | k2 **garbage** (`1701667150` for `3`) |\n| cross-module static call | OK | k5 clean compile error |\n\nSo the local binding is irrelevant; **the cycle is the trigger**, and `new` is\nthe only path that fails SILENTLY — the static-call path errors honestly.\n\n\n`compilation.rs:3872` already retries modules that fail, precisely for\n\"dependency ordering issues from cycles\", looping until a pass makes no\nprogress. **The value-wrap defeats it**: the module does not fail, so\n`try_compile_import` returns true and it is never re-queued. That makes the\nsilence the actual defect, not just an ugly symptom.\n\nBut note k5: the retry loop **cannot break a true 2-cycle** either — both\nmodules fail every pass, no progress, both become final failures. Real support\nneeds signature registration separated from body lowering. Fixing the value-wrap\nalone converts SIGSEGV/garbage into k5's clean error; it does NOT make cycles\nwork, and therefore does NOT by itself fix `Xml.parse`.\n\n\nA probe at that site shows it fires for real classes in **working** programs:\n\n```\nk2 (broken): [VALUEWRAP] class=Doc              kind=Some(\"Class { .. }\")\nk3 (works):  [VALUEWRAP] class=ArrayIterator    kind=Some(\"Class { .. }\")\n             [VALUEWRAP] class=StringIterator   kind=Some(\"Class { .. }\")\n```\n\n`Doc` is known to be `TypeKind::Class` when it is wrapped — the information is\nthere and thrown away — but so are `ArrayIterator`/`StringIterator`, whose wrap\nthe iteration-handle machinery tolerates. So \"refuse to wrap a `TypeKind::Class`\"\nwould change stdlib iterator construction. No local signal separates them;\n`class_alloc_sizes_by_name` is `None` for both. A discriminator has to come from\nwhether the class DECLARES a constructor, and the symbol table does not appear to\ncarry class members.\n\n\n`for (c in x)` over an `Xml` panics in cranelift-jit's\n`backend.rs:252` with `can't resolve symbol iterator`. Separate from 1 and 2:\n`scratchpad/xml2` avoids iteration entirely and still hits blocker 2.\n\n\nEvery \"obvious\" hypothesis here was wrong and was killed by a probe, not by\nargument: block-init, package, stdlib-vs-user, import depth, `using`, the enum\nabstract, the `Xml` param, interpolation, shadowing. The thing that finally\ndiscriminated was varying ONE name at a time across three otherwise-identical\nprojects. `RAYZOR_DEBUG_RECV=1` is the right first probe for any E0801 —\nit says outright whether the receiver is Dynamic.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_xml_parse_three_remaining_blockers` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}